plan Rules
title   Rules on machines: invariants, turns and terminals, judged by every run and measured by rule coverage
status  proposed
goal
    An app author writes what must always hold beside the machine it is about, in the machine's own typed tokens, and
    Flow checks it everywhere it already runs the machine: every Story, generated run and sweep. Rule coverage shows
    which rules no run exercised, so an unexercised rule is never mistaken for a passed one. A rule that is about the
    graph, a terminal state, is proved by `flow check` instead. Rules are claims, never guards: the core never calls
    one, and outside tests a rule runs only where its author enabled it.

    Today an app's own guarantees are hand-written checks inside single Stories (NUMBER-12's bounds filter), which the
    generator and sweeps never judge. Rules replace those with one declaration that every run judges.

    Every decision here is the user's (2026-11-19 and 2026-11-20). Not adopted, with why: `eventually` (Flow has no
    defined end of a run that ran its course; a Story may hold a call or leave a timer armed on purpose; a turns rule
    with `on` the turn that should clear a value covers most cases); `relations` across actors (it needs an actor-query
    API only to state a claim); `reachable` (the built-in unreachable-state warning and rule coverage cover it); a rule
    on where an action may be taken (drafted as `never`, `effects` and `allowed`; the word and the shape were not
    settled, and it is kept in code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/revisit.md); a `final` state that stops the
    actor (Flow actors' lifetimes belong to the host; subroutine exits already end a sub-flow); a `when` condition that
    coverage counts apart from the subject (kept simple; see @O5's limit).
outcomes
    O1  Rule kinds and what they read
        A machine declares rules in a `rules` block in its `machine()` callback, where its `S`, `E` and `O` tokens
        exist; no rule names a state, event or operation by string. Two kinds are judged by runs:

        - `invariants`: true at every publication.
        - `turns`: true of every accepted turn, given the snapshot before and after it. Named turns, not transitions:
          the rule is judged on every accepted turn, including turns that only update memory, and "transition" already
          means a handler's `target`.

        An entry is a function, or an object `{ check, in?, enabled? }` for an invariant and `{ check, on?, enabled? }`
        for a turns rule. `in: S.X` and `on: E.X` name the rule's subject: a rule is exercised only when its subject
        comes up, and a rule without one at every publication or accepted turn. `in` on a compound holds in it and every
        state below it, as `S.X.is` means. `on: E.X` matches every accepted turn of X, whatever sent it: a user, an
        `onContext` reaction, a schedule, an operation ending or a host's raise into a fragment.

        A rule reads what a guard reads except `context`: `state`, `memory`, `props`, `operations` and `schedules`. Only
        turns rules also read `context`, as the turn's guards saw it (`before.context`). `before` is the last snapshot
        published before the turn, which may follow publications that were not turns. A consumer's context changes a
        publication before its own state and memory react, so a rule comparing context with a memory copy of it breaks
        on correct code during that lag; a rule over context agrees only with guards that read context directly. A turns
        rule that names `on: E.X` also gets `event`, typed as X's payload; without `on` there is no `event`.

        Only accepted turns are judged by turns rules and counted for them. A denied or unhandled turn runs no handler,
        but it may still publish a lane change (@spec.Turn.R19): invariants judge that publication, turns rules skip it.
        A restore publishes but is not a turn, so invariants judge the restored snapshot and turns rules skip it; a pair
        across a reload is never judged, because saves coalesce and the last captured snapshot may be older than the
        last published one.

        ```ts
        // existing: definition, machine, S, E, snapped (stepping.ts)
        // NEW: rules, invariants, turns, in, on, check, enabled, event (in a turns check)
        // user-defined: StartedWhileHolding, SetLandsInBounds, CommitMovesByOne, CommitDiffersFromStart
        export const StepperMachine = machine(Stepper, ({ S, E }) => ({
          default: S.IDLE,
          on: {/* handlers, unchanged */},
          states: {/* states, unchanged */},
          rules: {
            invariants: {
              StartedWhileHolding: { in: S.HOLD_REPEAT, check: ({ memory }) => memory.started !== null },
            },
            turns: {
              SetLandsInBounds: {
                on: E.Set,
                enabled: import.meta.env.DEV,              // also runs in dev; tests judge it regardless
                check: ({ event, before, after }) =>
                  after.memory.value === (event.value === null ? null : snapped(before.memory.settings, event.value)),
              },
              // Every commit, whichever event made it (Release, Cancel, Blur, Key, WheelDue): no `on`.
              CommitMovesByOne: ({ before, after }) =>
                after.memory.lastCommit.seq === before.memory.lastCommit.seq ||
                after.memory.lastCommit.seq === before.memory.lastCommit.seq + 1,
              CommitDiffersFromStart: ({ before, after }) =>
                after.memory.lastCommit.seq === before.memory.lastCommit.seq ||
                after.memory.lastCommit.value !== (before.memory.started?.from ?? before.memory.value),
            },
          },
        }))

        // context, read by a turns rule, where Begin's guard reads `context.count` itself
        CartNotEmptyOnBegin: { on: E.Begin, check: ({ before }) => before.context.count > 0 },
        ```

        A value range such as "value stays within min and max" is not an invariant of this Stepper: `Configure` may
        narrow the bounds without re-clamping `value`. `SetLandsInBounds` states what is true instead.
    O2  Terminals
        `terminals` lists state tokens, leaves or compounds at any depth, that no edge leaves. A leaf allows only a
        self-transition; a compound allows moves inside its subtree. Handlers on ancestors count, and in a subroutine so
        do its exits. Every event source counts: user events, `onContext` reactions, schedules and late operation
        endings. Restarts are not edges: a new incarnation, a restore or a trait's `resetOn`. A terminal pins the state
        only; memory may still change, and a turns rule claims more.

        `flow check` proves terminals over the compiled graph: an edge out is a `TerminalLeft` error naming the handler
        and the state it can leave; an empty list or a state listed twice is refused; a listed state is never warned as
        likely stuck. Guard functions are opaque, so the proof counts any handler that may fire in the terminal; a
        state-token guard (`guard: [S.IDLE]`) narrows where its branch fires, and the error names the handler so the
        author can restructure it. Being about the graph, terminals are proved, never judged by runs; they have no
        token, and neither the switch (@O4, `off: "all"` included) nor `enabled` (@O6) applies to them.

        Terminals live in `rules`, not on the definition's states list: a terminal is a claim, not shape, and changes no
        behaviour. Flow does not stop an actor in a terminal state.

        ```ts
        // states: ["PENDING", { SHIPPED: ["IN_TRANSIT", "DELIVERED"] }, "REFUNDED", "CANCELLED"]
        rules: {
          terminals: [S.REFUNDED, S.CANCELLED, S.SHIPPED.DELIVERED],   // NEW: terminals
        },
        ```
    O3  Rule names and tokens
        Rules have their own namespace under `rules`, apart from the shared namespace of states, memory keys,
        operations, props, emits and trait instances. A name is unique across `invariants` and `turns` in one machine or
        fragment: a duplicate is a type error, and `machine()` also refuses it at construction for casts and plain
        JavaScript. Each rule is a typed token, `machine.rules.<Name>` or `fragment.rules.<Name>`.

        Typing the tokens changes `machine()`: today it returns a fixed `Machine<D>`, so it needs the body's type as a
        second parameter. A shared body typed through the constraint (a `Parameters<typeof machine<…>>` alias) or a
        machine annotated `Machine<typeof X>` loses the tokens; `Machine`'s second parameter defaults so such code still
        compiles, and its rules are reached by name only where the type is kept. A type spike showed the duplicate error
        and inferred check inputs can coexist; the build repeats it in `tests/types/` against the real `machine()`.
    O4  Judging in tests
        Every Story, generated run, reload sweep and pair sweep judges every rule with no extra call. A broken rule
        fails the run with the shortest Story that shows it (the generator's shrink), naming the rule, the snapshot
        before and after, and for a fragment copy the copy. When several rules break at one publication or turn, each
        gets its own record, invariants first and then in declaration order; the run fails on the first and the report
        lists every rule broken there.

        Checking is switchable and never silent. A Story's chained `.rules({ expectBroken, off })` holds for the whole
        Story wherever it is written, so Story helpers can add it; it is kept in the Story's plan as data beside its
        options, not as a step. `expectBroken` passes only if each named rule breaks at least once; a named rule never
        exercised fails as "never broke". After a rule's first break the run goes on, later breaks are not failures, and
        generated runs started from the Story treat it as off. `off` (a list of tokens, or `"all"`) is not judged and
        does not count as exercised. A rule in both lists is refused. Rule coverage lists every rule expected or off.
        Generated runs and sweeps started from a Story inherit its switch.

        ```ts
        // NEW: .rules, expectBroken, off, machine.rules.<Name>
        // A planted fault: a Stepper variant whose Set skips the clamp.
        export const setUnclamped = Story.machine(UnclampedStepperMachine, { id: "number-input/set-unclamped" })
          .rules({ expectBroken: [UnclampedStepperMachine.rules.SetLandsInBounds] })
          .send(Stepper.E.Configure({ settings: bounded }))
          .send(Stepper.E.Set({ value: 99 }))

        export const stressEdge = numberStory("number-input/stress-edge")
          .rules({ off: "all" })
          .send(quantity, Stepper.E.Configure({ settings: bounded }))
        ```

        Flow's own run rules (the runtime's guarantees, which no app can break and no machine declares) get the same
        three modes only in Flow's own test helper, named by the rule value, never by a string: `checked(run, { off:
        [everyCommitEndsOnce] })`. They are never public tokens and never in a Story's `.rules`. Their `breaches` and
        `breachesOf` are renamed `broken` and `brokenOf`, so one word, broken, is used everywhere.
    O5  Rule coverage
        The test run reports how often each rule was exercised and how many of all rules were, like code coverage. A
        rule no run exercised is listed as unseen, with why (its state never reached, its event never accepted, its
        fragment never placed), never as passed. Unseen is a measurement, not a failure: it never fails the suite, and
        nothing marks a rule as allowed to be unseen. Rules that are `off` are left out of the total; expected rules are
        counted and listed as expected. A fragment rule is one row per placement, its copies added together. Rule
        coverage is computed by the run and never stored, so `flow check` does not show it. It is its own report, apart
        from Story coverage (`story list --coverage`) and Inspection's unseen.

        ```text
        Rule coverage  12 of 14 exercised (86%)
          Number/Stepper.SetLandsInBounds     checked  seen 214 (on Set)
          Number/Stepper.CommitMovesByOne     checked  seen 1,930 turns
          Desk/Editor/uploads.CancelSticks    checked  seen 6 (copies a, b, c)
          unseen  Number/Stepper.StartedWhileHolding    in HOLD_REPEAT, never reached
          unseen  Shop/Pay/card.NumberBeforeCheck       CardEntry never placed
        ```

        The limit: a rule counts its subject, not the condition inside its check. "If cancelled, it stays cancelled"
        counts every turn of its subject even when nothing was ever cancelled. The exact format is settled in the build.
    O6  Outside tests
        Rules stay inline in `machine()`, so every Story, sweep and tool finds them with the machine and nothing needs
        registering on the app or the Runtime. Outside tests a rule runs only if its own `enabled` flag is true; it is
        off by default, and a rule written as a bare function has none, so it never runs outside tests. `enabled` is a
        boolean fixed when the module loads (an env check such as `import.meta.env.DEV`), so `flow explain` can show it.
        Tests judge every rule whatever `enabled` says; there the switch (@O4) decides.

        The host judges enabled rules after each publication or accepted turn, keeping the previous published snapshot
        as `before`. A broken rule becomes a `RuleBroken` evidence record that inspectors and logs receive, with
        `Flow.private` memory fields redacted, and the app carries on: the publication has happened, so nothing is
        thrown. The core never calls a rule. An author who wants rules out of a production bundle can wrap the block in
        a build-time constant (`rules: import.meta.env.DEV ? {...} : undefined`); that is user code, not Flow API.
    O7  Fragments
        A `trait` or `subroutine` body holds the same `rules` block as `machine()`. Placing the fragment brings its
        rules, and a host can never remove or weaken one. A fragment's rules read only what the fragment declares: its
        own memory, operations and schedules, and for a subroutine its own states (a trait has none). Never props,
        context or the host's data, which a fragment cannot declare or see. Subjects are its own `S` and `E` tokens,
        `hostEvents` included; `on` matches a host's raise into the fragment.

        Rules are judged while the incarnation is live, on each placement and each `.each` copy separately; a frozen
        `.resumable()` trait is not judged. Turns rules skip a copy's entry, reset (`resetOn`) and exit, as they skip a
        restore: there is no before or after of the same copy. `Story.fragment` judges them alone against its stand-in
        host; host Stories judge them where placed. Rule coverage names the placement
        (`Desk/Editor/uploads.CancelSticks`), and a broken rule also names the copy (`uploads[a]`). A fragment token
        (`Upload.rules.CancelSticks`) switches every placement. Built with fragments and `Story.fragment`.

        ```ts
        // existing (Plan shape): trait, definition, machine, Flow.input, Flow.initial, .each, raise,
        //   Story.fragment, enter, leave, Story.machine
        // NEW: rules in a trait body
        // user-defined: Upload, CancelSticks, RefNeverChanges, Editor, EditorMachine, Attach, CancelAll,
        //   attachments, uploads, uploadAlone, twoUploads
        export const Upload = trait(
          {
            id: "Upload",
            events: { Cancel: {} },
            memory: {
              ref: Flow.input(Schema.String),
              cancelled: Flow.initial(Schema.Boolean, false),
            },
          },
          () => ({
            on: { Cancel: { target: "self", updateMemory: () => ({ cancelled: true }) } },
            rules: {
              turns: {
                CancelSticks: ({ before, after }) => !before.memory.cancelled || after.memory.cancelled,
                RefNeverChanges: ({ before, after }) => before.memory.ref === after.memory.ref,
              },
            },
          }),
        )

        export const Editor = definition({
          id: "Desk/Editor",
          states: ["EDITING"],
          events: { Attach: { ref: Schema.String }, CancelAll: {} },
          memory: {
            attachments: Flow.initial(Schema.Array(Schema.Struct({ ref: Schema.String })), []),
          },
          traits: { uploads: Upload.each({ of: "attachments", by: "ref", max: 10 }) },
        })

        export const EditorMachine = machine(Editor, ({ S, E }) => ({
          default: S.EDITING,
          on: {
            Attach: {
              updateMemory: ({ event, memory }) => ({ attachments: [...memory.attachments, { ref: event.ref }] }),
            },
            CancelAll: { raise: [E.uploads.Cancel] },
          },
        }))

        // The fragment alone: Upload.CancelSticks and Upload.RefNeverChanges, seen 2 each.
        export const uploadAlone = Story.fragment(Upload, { id: "upload/alone" })
          .enter({ ref: "file-1" })
          .send(Upload.E.Cancel({}))
          .send(Upload.E.Cancel({}))
          .leave()

        // The host: Desk/Editor/uploads.CancelSticks and .RefNeverChanges, seen 2 each (copies a, b, one raise each).
        export const twoUploads = Story.machine(EditorMachine, { id: "editor/two-uploads" })
          .send(Editor.E.Attach({ ref: "a" }))
          .send(Editor.E.Attach({ ref: "b" }))
          .send(Editor.E.CancelAll({}))
        ```
    O8  Tools
        `flow explain` lists each machine's rules and their `enabled`. `flow check` proves terminals (@O2) and warns
        `RuleSubjectUnreachable` when the compiled graph cannot reach a rule's `in` state or no reachable state handles
        its `on` event. Guards make the graph an over-approximation, so that warning is never false; it is a warning,
        not an error, so a machine mid-edit still checks, and it separates "impossible" from "not tested yet", which
        rule coverage alone cannot. The test reporter prints rule coverage (@O5).
verification
    V1  Type tests prove rule tokens, inputs and subjects against the real `machine()`: `machine.rules.<Name>` exists
        for each rule and not for others; a duplicate name across kinds is a type error; `event` is typed from `on` and
        absent without it; `in` on a turns rule and `on` on an invariant are errors; `context` is readable in turns
        rules only; each check's input is inferred; a shared body and a `Machine<typeof X>` annotation still compile.
    V2  Stories prove every run judges: a planted broken invariant and a planted broken turns rule each fail a Story,
        a generated run (with its shortest Story) and the reload sweep; denied and unhandled turns are not judged by
        turns rules while their lane changes are judged by invariants; a restore is judged by invariants only; several
        rules broken at once each get a record, in order.
    V3  Stories prove the switch: `expectBroken` passes only when the rule breaks and fails as "never broke" when it
        is never exercised, `off` skips and is listed, a rule in both lists is refused, and a Story's switch reaches the
        runs generated from it.
    V4  Tests prove rule coverage: counts per rule and subject, unseen with its reason, `off` left out of the total,
        and that unseen never fails.
    V5  `flow check` tests prove terminals (an ancestor handler, a late ending and a subroutine exit each give
        `TerminalLeft`; restarts, self-transitions and a branch whose state-token guard excludes the terminal do not; an
        empty or duplicate list is refused) and `RuleSubjectUnreachable`; `flow explain` lists rules and `enabled`.
    V6  Runtime tests prove `enabled` outside tests: off by default and for bare functions, a broken enabled rule
        records `RuleBroken` with private fields redacted and the app carries on, and the core calls no rule.
    V7  NUMBER-12's hand check and at least one other become declared rules; each rule kind and terminals has a
        planted fault caught by `nub run faults --only`; `machine()` refuses a duplicate rule name passed past the
        types; the run rules' `broken` rename is complete.
    V8  With fragments: per-copy judging, turns rules skipping a copy's entry, reset and exit, `on` matching a host's
        raise, coverage by placement, `Story.fragment` judging alone, and a host unable to remove a fragment's rule.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        The suite and bench cost of rules, measured with rules on and off before a budget is set. Whether an operation
        row is busy in the same publication as the commit that started it, which rules over `operations` depend on.
        Switching one fragment placement on its own, as a later extension. The rule coverage format.
